{"id":"scheduling-eviction-pod-overhead","corpus":"k8s","title":"Pod Overhead","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/pod-overhead/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Pod Overhead"},{"kind":"p","text":"When you run a Pod on a Node, the Pod itself takes an amount of system resources. These resources are additional to the resources needed to run the container(s) inside the Pod. In Kubernetes, Pod Overhead is a way to account for the resources consumed by the Pod infrastructure on top of the container requests & limits."},{"kind":"p","text":"In Kubernetes, the Pod's overhead is set at admission time according to the overhead associated with the Pod's RuntimeClass."},{"kind":"p","text":"A pod's overhead is considered in addition to the sum of container resource requests when scheduling a Pod. Similarly, the kubelet will include the Pod overhead when sizing the Pod cgroup, and when carrying out Pod eviction ranking."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Configuring Pod overhead"},{"kind":"p","text":"You need to make sure a RuntimeClass is utilized which defines the overhead field."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Usage example"},{"kind":"p","text":"To work with Pod overhead, you need a RuntimeClass that defines the overhead field. As an example, you could use the following RuntimeClass definition with a virtualization container runtime (in this example, Kata Containers combined with the Firecracker virtual machine monitor) that uses around 120MiB per Pod for the virtual machine and the guest OS:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Workloads which are created which specify the kata-fc RuntimeClass handler will take the memory and cpu overheads into account for resource quota calculations, node scheduling, as well as Pod cgroup sizing."},{"kind":"p","text":"Consider running the given example workload, test-pod:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If only limits are specified in the pod definition, kubelet will deduce requests from those limits and set them to be the same as the defined limits."},{"kind":"p","text":"At admission time the RuntimeClass admission controller updates the workload's PodSpec to include the overhead as described in the RuntimeClass. If the PodSpec already has this field defined, the Pod will be rejected. In the given example, since only the RuntimeClass name is specified, the admission controller mutates the Pod to include an overhead."},{"kind":"p","text":"After the RuntimeClass admission controller has made modifications, you can check the updated Pod overhead value:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output is:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If a ResourceQuota is defined, the sum of container requests as well as the overhead field are counted."},{"kind":"p","text":"When the kube-scheduler is deciding which node should run a new Pod, the scheduler considers that Pod's overhead as well as the sum of container requests for that Pod. For this example, the scheduler adds the requests and the overhead, then looks for a node that has 2.25 CPU and 320 MiB of memory available."},{"kind":"p","text":"Once a Pod is scheduled to a node, the kubelet on that node creates a new cgroup for the Pod. It is within this pod that the underlying container runtime will create containers."},{"kind":"p","text":"If the resource has a limit defined for each container (Guaranteed QoS or Burstable QoS with limits defined), the kubelet will set an upper limit for the pod cgroup associated with that resource (cpu.cfs_quota_us for CPU and memory.limit_in_bytes memory). This upper limit is based on the sum of the container limits plus the overhead defined in the PodSpec."},{"kind":"p","text":"For CPU, if the Pod is Guaranteed or Burstable QoS, the kubelet will set cpu.shares based on the sum of container requests plus the overhead defined in the PodSpec."},{"kind":"p","text":"Looking at our example, verify the container requests for the workload:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The total container requests are 2000m CPU and 200MiB of memory:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Check this against what is observed by the node:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output shows requests for 2250m CPU, and for 320MiB of memory. The requests include Pod overhead:"},{"kind":"h2","text":"Verify Pod cgroup limits"},{"kind":"p","text":"Check the Pod's memory cgroups on the node where the workload is running. In the following example, crictl is used on the node, which provides a CLI for CRI-compatible container runtimes. This is an advanced example to show Pod overhead behavior, and it is not expected that users should need to check cgroups directly on the node."},{"kind":"p","text":"First, on the particular node, determine the Pod identifier:"},{"kind":"p","text":"From this, you can determine the cgroup path for the Pod:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The resulting cgroup path includes the Pod's pause container. The Pod level cgroup is one directory above."},{"kind":"p","text":"In this specific case, the pod cgroup path is kubepods/podd7f4b509-cf94-4951-9417-d1087c92a5b2. Verify the Pod level cgroup setting for memory:"}],"lemmas":{"pods":["pod","pods"],"whens":["whens","when"],"runs":["run","runs"],"ran":["ran","run"],"nodes":["node","nod","nodes"],"themselves":["themselves","themself"],"takes":["take","takes"],"takeing":["takeing","take"],"taking":["taking","take"],"taken":["taken","take"],"took":["took","take"],"amounts":["amount","amounts"],"amounted":["amounted","amount"],"amounting":["amounting","amount"],"systems":["system","systems"],"resources":["resource","resources"],"additionals":["additionals","additional"],"needed":["needed","need"],"neededs":["neededs","needed"],"needs":["need","needs"],"needing":["needing","need"],"insides":["inside","insides"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"accounts":["account","accounts"],"accounting":["accounting","account"],"accounted":["accounted","account"],"consumed":["consumed","consume"],"consumeds":["cons